BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 9 — AI-First Compiler Features
BLOCK|p||This chapter explains Cluster-lang's unique compiler features designed specifically for AI models (LLMs) and developers to achieve maximum execution speeds and automated debugging.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. The Dynamic `any` Type with C++20 Variant Backing
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang provides an `any` type for variables whose types can change at runtime. Unlike slow interpreted languages, Cluster-lang implements this with high-performance stack-allocated C++20 variants (`ClusterAny`), avoiding costly heap allocations and garbage collection.
BLOCK|code|python|x: any = 42
put x          // Prints 42

x = "Hello, Cluster!"
put x          // Prints Hello, Cluster!
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Static Type Promotion Pass (Zero Overhead)
BLOCK|p||If a variable declared as `any` is assigned uniform types (e.g. only numbers), the Cluster compiler performs **Static Type Promotion**.
BLOCK|code|python|y: any = 3.14
y = 1.5
BLOCK|p||At build time, the typechecker recognizes that `y` is only ever assigned double-precision floating-point values. It optimizes `y`'s transpiled type to a native C++ `double`, executing at full hardware speed without any runtime type-check penalty.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Auto-Import Resolution
BLOCK|p||To prevent compilation failures and simplify code writing, the compiler automatically exposes and resolves standard package namespaces:
BLOCK|list||`db` (Databases)
BLOCK|list||`fs` (Filesystem)
BLOCK|list||`json` (JSON parser & serializer)
BLOCK|list||`sys` (System variables and processes)
BLOCK|list||`tensor` (Tensor operations)
BLOCK|p||You can call standard methods immediately without writing manual `import` headers:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    content := fs.read("data.txt")
    put content
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Structured JSON Diagnostics (`--json-errors`)
BLOCK|p||For AI code editors and automated build agents, the compiler supports a structured output flag.
BLOCK|p||Executing compilation with the `--json-errors` flag formats parser syntax and typechecker diagnostics into a structured JSON array:
BLOCK|code|bash|cluster my_code.cl --json-errors
BLOCK|p||**Example output:**
BLOCK|code|json|[
  {
    "line": 12,
    "column": 5,
    "type": "TypeError",
    "message": "Cannot assign string to integer variable 'count'"
  }
]
BLOCK|p||This enables LLMs and IDE extensions to parse and automatically correct errors instantly.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|5. Global `input()` function
BLOCK|p||For receiving console input from users, Cluster-lang provides a simple, global built-in function `input(prompt)` that matches standard developer conventions:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    name := input("Enter name: ")
    put "Hello, " + name
BLOCK|p||The prompt is displayed on standard output, and the function reads a single line from standard input.
BLOCK|hr||
